WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.851 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You're making a snack mix.

00:00:01.971 --> 00:00:05.979 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One scoop fills half a cup, and another fills a
quarter of a cup.

00:00:06.099 --> 00:00:09.614 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Will they fill the cup, spill over, or leave
some space?

00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:16.450 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Today, we're learning to add and subtract
fractions using visual models and number
strategies.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:20.057 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The secret is working with equal-sized parts.

00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:25.004 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll make the parts the same size when the
denominators are related.

00:00:25.233 --> 00:00:29.829 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll add or subtract carefully, and simplify
your answer when you can.

00:00:30.033 --> 00:00:35.547 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You'll use a model, a number line or an estimate
to explain why your answer makes sense.

00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:37.331 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First, choose one whole.

00:00:37.451 --> 00:00:40.167 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Its parts must be equal in size.

00:00:40.287 --> 00:00:44.924 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The outline of the model shows the entire whole,
not just the coloured share.

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:48.649 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Here, three equal parts are chosen out of four.

00:00:48.769 --> 00:00:52.538 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The top number, the numerator, counts chosen
parts.

00:00:52.658 --> 00:00:57.273 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The bottom number, the denominator, tells how
many equal parts make the whole.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.593 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
What is two sevenths plus three sevenths?

00:01:00.713 --> 00:01:05.639 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Think about the size of each piece, then predict
the number of pieces altogether.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:11.321 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
First, use matching denominators.

00:01:11.441 --> 00:01:14.974 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two sevenths plus three sevenths makes five
sevenths.

00:01:15.333 --> 00:01:18.592 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We add the numerators and keep the denominator.

00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:22.216 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
We are still counting sevenths, not fourteenths.

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:28.977 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Adding denominators would rename our pieces as
fourteenths, even though we never cut the pieces
smaller.

00:01:29.097 --> 00:01:32.593 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Keep the piece size, and count the chosen
pieces.

00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:38.709 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For half a cup plus a quarter of a cup, what
size of piece could you use for both amounts?

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:40.284 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Predict the total.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:48.719 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For one half plus one quarter, the denominators
are related: four is a multiple of two.

00:01:48.839 --> 00:01:51.296 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Rename the half as two quarters.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:54.385 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Now add two quarters and one quarter.

00:01:54.505 --> 00:01:56.834 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The result is three quarters.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.446 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two quarters plus one quarter is three quarters.

00:02:00.566 --> 00:02:05.397 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
That's more than half a cup but less than a full
cup, which agrees with the picture.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.942 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Subtraction follows the same idea.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:13.902 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For three quarters minus one eighth, rename
three quarters as six eighths.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.125 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Take away one eighth.

00:02:16.245 --> 00:02:17.893 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Five eighths remain.

00:02:18.013 --> 00:02:20.168 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The denominator stays eight.

00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:24.400 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Six eighths with one eighth removed leaves five
eighths.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.836 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The result is smaller than three quarters, so
our subtraction passes a quick sense check.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.478 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
On a number line, begin at six eighths and move
back one eighth.

00:02:34.598 --> 00:02:36.649 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
You land on five eighths.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:38.934 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Simplify when you can.

00:02:39.054 --> 00:02:43.911 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One sixth plus three sixths is four sixths,
which equals two thirds.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.734 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Mixed numbers can make another whole.

00:02:46.854 --> 00:02:50.997 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One and four fifths plus two fifths is one and
six fifths.

00:02:51.367 --> 00:02:53.917 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Regroup five fifths as one whole.

00:02:54.037 --> 00:02:56.684 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The answer is two and one fifth.

00:02:57.067 --> 00:03:00.970 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
For several fractions, rename them with a common
denominator.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:07.405 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
One half plus one quarter minus one eighth
becomes four eighths plus two eighths minus one
eighth.

00:03:07.767 --> 00:03:12.328 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Four plus two minus one is five, so the result
is five eighths.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.540 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
What is two thirds minus one sixth?

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:22.682 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Two thirds is four sixths.

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:27.010 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Four sixths minus one sixth is three sixths, or
one half.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:30.605 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Our goal was to add and subtract fractions.

00:03:30.725 --> 00:03:35.419 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Make the parts match, combine or remove them,
then simplify and check.

00:03:35.539 --> 00:03:38.835 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
What would you draw first for your next
calculation?

00:03:38.955 --> 00:03:41.955 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
Try the fraction practice on Math Magic.

00:03:42.075 --> 00:03:46.255 line:1% position:50% align:center size:88%
The separate L C M video is optional extension
work.
